# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The opening comparison (22:1–2). “And Jesus answered and spake unto them again by parables, and said, The kingdom of heaven is like unto a certain king, which made a marriage for his son.” The comparison is to the kingdom of heaven, “like unto a certain king, which made a marriage for his son.” The story begins with a king and a marriage he has made for his son.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The first sending (22:3). “And sent forth his servants to call them that were bidden to the wedding: and they would not come.” The king sends servants to call those who are “bidden.” The text says “they would not come.” It does not say how many were bidden, and it gives no reason for the refusal in this verse.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The second sending (22:4). “Again, he sent forth other servants, saying, Tell them which are bidden, Behold, I have prepared my dinner: my oxen and my fatlings are killed, and all things are ready: come unto the marriage.” The king sends other servants with a message. The message has four parts: the dinner is prepared; the oxen and fatlings are killed; all things are ready; come unto the marriage.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The answer to the second sending (22:5–6). “But they made light of it, and went their ways, one to his farm, another to his merchandise: And the remnant took his servants, and entreated them spitefully, and slew them.” Three things are told. They made light of it and went their ways, one to a farm and another to merchandise. The remnant took the servants, entreated them spitefully, and slew them. The text does not say how many servants were taken, and it does not say whether they came from the first or the second sending.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The king’s answer (22:7). “But when the king heard thereof, he was wroth: and he sent forth his armies, and destroyed those murderers, and burned up their city.” The king hears, is wroth, sends armies, destroys “those murderers,” and burns “their city.” The text does not name the city.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The turn (22:8–9). “Then saith he to his servants, The wedding is ready, but they which were bidden were not worthy. Go ye therefore into the highways, and as many as ye shall find, bid to the marriage.” The king says two things of the wedding and those bidden: the wedding is ready, and those bidden were not worthy. Then he gives a command with “therefore”: go into the highways, and bid as many as you find.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The wedding furnished (22:10). “So those servants went out into the highways, and gathered together all as many as they found, both bad and good: and the wedding was furnished with guests.” The servants do as they were told. They gather “all as many as they found, both bad and good,” and the wedding is furnished with guests.'))
A(('p', '3.8 The king comes in (22:11–12). “And when the king came in to see the guests, he saw there a man which had not on a wedding garment: And he saith unto him, Friend, how camest thou in hither not having a wedding garment? And he was speechless.” The king comes in to see the guests. He sees one man without a wedding garment. He asks him a question, and the man is speechless. The king’s question treats the missing garment as something to be explained.'))
A(('p', '3.9 The sentence (22:13). “Then said the king to the servants, Bind him hand and foot, and take him away, and cast him into outer darkness; there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.” The king commands the servants. The command has three parts: bind him hand and foot, take him away, cast him into outer darkness. The verse ends with a statement: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.'))
A(('p', '3.10 The closing saying (22:14). “For many are called, but few are chosen.” The saying begins with “For.” It comes directly after the story and is not itself a scene. It names two things, the many who are called and the few who are chosen.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 22:2', '“a certain king, which made a marriage for his son”', 'The king and the marriage for his son.'],
    ['Matt 22:3', '“they would not come”', 'The first sending is refused.'],
    ['Matt 22:4', '“all things are ready: come unto the marriage”', 'The second sending, with its message.'],
    ['Matt 22:5–6', '“made light of it”; “entreated them spitefully, and slew them”', 'Indifference, then violence.'],
    ['Matt 22:7', '“destroyed those murderers, and burned up their city”', 'The king’s answer.'],
    ['Matt 22:8–9', '“they which were bidden were not worthy”', 'The turn to the highways.'],
    ['Matt 22:10', '“both bad and good”', 'The wedding furnished with guests.'],
    ['Matt 22:11–12', '“Friend, how camest thou in hither not having a wedding garment?”', 'The king’s question; the man is speechless.'],
    ['Matt 22:13', '“cast him into outer darkness”', 'The sentence.'],
    ['Matt 22:14', '“many are called, but few are chosen”', 'The closing saying.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.11 What the story contains. The text names a king, a son, a marriage, servants sent twice, those bidden, a dinner, a farm and merchandise, the remnant, armies, a city, highways, guests both bad and good, a man, a wedding garment, outer darkness, and weeping and gnashing of teeth. The action moves from an invitation, to a refusal, to a destruction, to a second invitation, to a filled wedding, to one man called to account.'))
A(('p', '3.12 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the king is. Jesus does not say who the son is, and the son never appears in the story. Jesus does not say who the servants are. Jesus does not say who those bidden are. Jesus does not say who the remnant is, or what city is burned. Jesus does not say who the people of the highways are. Jesus does not say who the man is, or what the wedding garment is, or where such a garment comes from. Jesus does not say who the servants of 22:13 are, and the text calls them “servants” without saying whether they are the servants sent out earlier. The story gives a wedding prepared, an invitation refused, a wedding filled, and a guest sent out, and it stops at the closing saying.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. Matthew records no explanation of the wedding and no request for one. The next verse after the closing saying turns to the Pharisees and their council (22:15). This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 The one saying Jesus adds. After the story Jesus says, “For many are called, but few are chosen” (22:14). The saying names the many who are called and the few who are chosen. It does not say who the many are, who the few are, who calls, who chooses, or which part of the story it draws on. It is recorded here as a saying and not as an identification.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not identify. The parable names many parts of the story and leaves each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the king. Jesus does not identify the son. Jesus does not identify the servants of the first sending, the servants of the second sending, or the servants of 22:13. Jesus does not identify those bidden. Jesus does not identify the remnant, the armies, the murderers or the city. Jesus does not identify the highways or the people found there. Jesus does not identify the guests, the man without a garment, or the garment. Jesus does not identify the outer darkness or the weeping and gnashing of teeth. Jesus does not identify the called or the chosen.'))
