A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the king is a certain person, that those bidden are a certain people, that the highways are a certain mission, that the city is a certain city, or that the garment is a certain thing is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What the text does say. The text says that the kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who made a marriage for his son; that he sent servants twice to those bidden; that they would not come, made light of it, and the remnant slew his servants; that the king sent armies, destroyed those murderers and burned their city; that he then sent servants to the highways to bid as many as they found; that the wedding was furnished with guests, both bad and good; that a man without a wedding garment was questioned, was speechless, and was cast into outer darkness; and that many are called, but few are chosen. These statements are made by the story itself and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for this parable, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus says the kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who made a marriage for his son, sent to those bidden, was refused, sent to the highways, and found the wedding furnished with guests, among whom one man was called to account. The meaning is in what the story shows of the invitation, the refusal, the filling of the wedding and the scrutiny of a guest.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the kingdom of heaven.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The Kingdom summons is ready.** The message is, “all things are ready: come unto the marriage” (22:4), and the king says again, “The wedding is ready” (22:8). What is offered is prepared before anyone is told to come.',
    '**Refusal has consequences.** Those bidden “would not come” (22:3) and “made light of it” (22:5). The king is “wroth,” and he destroys those murderers and burns their city (22:7). The king’s own word is that “they which were bidden were not worthy” (22:8).',
    '**The invitation expands outward.** When those bidden do not come, the king says, “Go ye therefore into the highways, and as many as ye shall find, bid to the marriage” (22:9). The servants gather “all as many as they found, both bad and good,” and “the wedding was furnished with guests” (22:10). The refusal does not leave the wedding empty.',
    '**Presumption does not guarantee participation.** A man is among the guests, and the king asks him, “Friend, how camest thou in hither not having a wedding garment?” (22:12). Being present at the wedding does not end the king’s scrutiny of a guest.',
    '**Being called and being chosen are not the same.** The closing saying says, “many are called, but few are chosen” (22:14). The story shows many called, a wedding furnished, and one man removed.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The people Matthew names in this scene are the chief priests and the elders of the people (21:23), the Pharisees (21:45), and the multitude (21:46). They had heard that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17). They were meant to understand that the kingdom of heaven has a marriage prepared by a king, that an invitation can be refused and that refusal has a cost, that the invitation can go to others, and that being among the guests does not remove the king’s question. Matthew does not say that those bidden in the story are these persons, and Jesus does not identify those bidden.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the reversal of the story’s first expectation. The wedding was made ready for those bidden, and they would not come. The same wedding is then filled from the highways. The force is also in the last scene, where a guest who is present is asked to account for himself. The parable asks the hearer to see that an invitation can be refused, widened and examined.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The Kingdom summons is ready. Refusal has consequences. The invitation expands outward. Presumption does not guarantee participation. Many are called, and few are chosen. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the king, the son, those bidden, the highways, the guests, the man or the garment represents anyone or anything. The garment is not made into a doctrine here: Matthew does not say what it is, where it comes from or whether it was given.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The wedding is one of the parables Matthew records on the day he describes in chapters 21 and 22. It is kept apart from its neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 14:16–24', '“A certain man made a great supper, and bade many”', 'The Great Banquet. A man, one servant, a supper, three excuses, streets and lanes, highways and hedges. No king, no son, no violence, no armies and no garment. It stands in its own study.'],
    ['Matt 21:33–41', '“a certain householder, which planted a vineyard”', 'The Wicked Tenants. A householder, a vineyard, husbandmen, servants and a son who is killed. It is told just before the wedding and is not an explanation of it.'],
    ['Matt 21:28–32', '“A certain man had two sons”', 'The Two Sons. A man and two sons, a vineyard, a command to work. The sons of that story are not the son of 22:2.'],
    ['Matt 25:1–13', '“went in with him to the marriage”', 'The Ten Virgins. A marriage again, but a bridegroom, lamps and a shut door, with no king and no servants.'],
    ['Matt 8:12; 25:30', '“outer darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth”', 'The same words appear in other settings. They are not an explanation of 22:13, and 22:13 is not an explanation of them.'],
    ['Matt 20:16', '“many be called, but few chosen”', 'Nearly the same words in another setting. They are not an explanation of 22:14, and 22:14 is not an explanation of them.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The wedding is told in Matthew in the temple, after the question about authority and after the Wicked Tenants (21:23, 33–45). The Great Banquet is told in Luke at a meal, in the house of one of the chief Pharisees on the sabbath day (14:1), after a guest says, “Blessed is he that shall eat bread in the kingdom of God” (14:15). A shared word such as “bidden” or “supper” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The wedding has a king, his son, many servants, those bidden, the remnant, armies, guests and a man without a garment. The Great Banquet has “a certain man,” one servant, three who make excuses, and a master of the house. The Wicked Tenants has a householder, husbandmen, servants and a son. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The wedding moves from a sending, to a refusal with violence, to a destruction, to the highways, to a filled wedding, to a man cast out. The Great Banquet moves from a supper and a sending, to excuses, to streets and lanes, to highways and hedges, and ends with a statement about the men who were bidden. None of those movements is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, the inspection of the guest and the wedding garment belong to Matthew 22 only. They are not read into Luke 14. Second, the refusals of Matthew 22:5–6, the farm, the merchandise and the slaying, are not Luke’s excuses of 14:18–20, and Luke’s excuses are not read into Matthew. Third, “the highways” of 22:9 are not “the streets and lanes” or the “highways and hedges” of Luke 14, and the “bad and good” of 22:10 are not the “poor, and the maimed, and the halt, and the blind” of Luke 14:21. Fourth, the servants and the son of the Wicked Tenants are not the servants and the son of 22:2–6.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (10:5–7). The wedding belongs to that ministry. The Kingdom it speaks of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel, and Matthew places the parable in the temple among the teaching of that day (21:23; 22:1).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The kingdom of heaven that Jesus compares to a king’s marriage for his son is the Kingdom He announced during His earthly ministry. It is not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The king, the son, the marriage, the dinner, the guests and the garment are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The sending, the coming, the refusing and the wearing or not wearing of a garment are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The text does not present the garment as a requirement for salvation, and it is not turned into one here. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of response.** The message is “all things are ready: come” (22:4). A reader learns that what is ready calls for an answer and not for delay.',
    '**A lesson of weight.** Those bidden “made light of it” (22:5). A reader learns not to treat a summons from one who has the right to give it as a small thing.',
    '**A lesson against presumption.** A guest is present and is asked how he came in (22:12). A reader learns that standing near what is offered is not the same as being ready for it.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** The guest is speechless (22:12). A reader learns that he may have no answer when he is asked to account for himself.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s marriage and feast are not turned into the Body’s hope, and the king’s question is not turned into a requirement laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'The kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who made a marriage for his son. Those bidden would not come, made light of it, and slew his servants, and the king destroyed those murderers and burned their city. His servants then bid all they found in the highways, both bad and good, and the wedding was furnished with guests. A man without a wedding garment was asked how he came in, was speechless, and was cast into outer darkness. Jesus ends with “For many are called, but few are chosen.” He gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that the Kingdom summons is ready, that refusal has consequences, that the invitation goes outward, and that presumption does not guarantee a place. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the king, the son, those bidden, the guests, the city or the garment are, or that makes coming or wearing a garment a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a king makes a marriage for his son; servants are sent twice; the answer is refusal and violence; the king destroys those murderers; servants go to the highways; the wedding is furnished; one man without a garment is cast out; many are called, but few are chosen.',
    '**The meaning:** the Kingdom summons is ready; refusal has consequences; the invitation expands outward; presumption does not guarantee participation.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the king, the son, those bidden, the guests, the city or the garment; nothing carried in from Luke 14 or the parables beside it.',
    '**The present lesson:** response, weight, no presumption and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

