A(('h1', S13))
A(('p', '13.1 Related passages. Read each in its own setting and keep it apart from this parable.'))
A(('bul', [
    'Luke 14:15–24: the Great Banquet, in its own setting.',
    'Matthew 21:28–32: the Two Sons.',
    'Matthew 21:33–46: the Wicked Tenants, and the reaction that follows.',
    'Matthew 25:1–13: the Ten Virgins.',
    'Matthew 8:12 and 25:30: “outer darkness,” each in its own setting.',
    'Matthew 20:16: “many be called, but few chosen,” in its own setting.',
    'Matthew 4:17 and 10:5–7: what was preached before the temple teaching.',
]))
A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Great Banquet, the Wicked Tenants, the Two Sons, the Laborers in the Vineyard and the Ten Virgins are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This parable makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus compares the kingdom of heaven to a king who made a marriage for his son. Those bidden would not come, and the king destroyed those murderers. Servants then gathered all they found in the highways, both bad and good, and the wedding was furnished with guests. One man without a wedding garment was asked how he came in, was speechless, and was cast into outer darkness. Jesus ends with “For many are called, but few are chosen.” He gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that the summons is ready, that refusal has consequences, that the invitation goes outward, and that presumption does not guarantee a place.'))
A(('pk', 'Write the synthesis in your own words in five to seven sentences. In it:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Tell the story in its order.',
    'Say plainly which parts Jesus does not identify.',
    'State what the comparison says about the kingdom of heaven.',
    'State where Jesus told it and what He was preaching.',
    'State one principle of conduct for today.',
    'End with one sentence naming what the parable does not say.',
]))
A(('call', 'Boundaries to Remember',
   'No explanation from Jesus, so no identity for the king, the son, those bidden, the guests, the city or the garment. No detail carried in from Luke 14 or the parables beside it. Coming, bidding and wearing a garment are actions in a story, not steps of salvation. The Body of Christ is none of the parts of this story. The lessons are principles of conduct, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
